WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.520
- [Kim] Hi this is Kim from Kahn Academy.

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:05.840
Today we're learning more
about Baker versus Carr,

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.930
a landmark Supreme Court
case decided in 1962.

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:11.910
Baker versus Carr grappled with an

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.770
incredibly important issue.

00:00:13.770 --> 00:00:17.650
Whether one person's vote is
equal to another person's vote.

00:00:17.650 --> 00:00:20.140
That seems pretty
straightforward to us now,

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.180
but as we'll learn in this video,

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:25.580
before Baker versus Carr,
it wasn't always the case.

00:00:25.580 --> 00:00:27.930
To learn more, I talked with two experts.

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:30.570
Theodore Olson, is a
partner at the law firm

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.530
of Gibson, Dunn, and served as

00:00:32.530 --> 00:00:37.363
Solicitor General of the United
States from 2001 to 2004.

00:00:37.363 --> 00:00:39.950
Guy-Uriel Charles, is the
Edward and Ellen Schwarzman

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.210
Professor of Law, and
the founding director

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:45.740
of the Duke Law Center on
Law, Race, and Politics.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.000
Mr. Olson, could you kind
of set the stage for us?

00:00:49.000 --> 00:00:52.540
What was the overall historical
and political context

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:56.280
that led up to the issues
of Baker versus Carr?

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:59.230
- [Olson] Well, it is a very,
very important decision.

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:01.910
Earl Warren, who was
Chief Justice at the time

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:04.267
the case was decided, later said that

00:01:04.267 --> 00:01:06.812
it was one of the most important decisions

00:01:06.812 --> 00:01:10.089
of his tenure as Chief Justice.

00:01:10.089 --> 00:01:13.700
Prior to the decision in Baker versus Carr

00:01:13.700 --> 00:01:15.971
and a couple of subsequent decisions,

00:01:15.971 --> 00:01:19.489
legislatures throughout the country were

00:01:19.489 --> 00:01:24.122
organized in a way based upon geography

00:01:24.122 --> 00:01:29.122
and other considerations
rather than population.

00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.280
We take the phrase one person,
one vote for granted now.

00:01:34.280 --> 00:01:38.160
But prior to that time,
rural counties particularly,

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:41.613
all over the United States
dominated state legislators,

00:01:41.613 --> 00:01:45.520
and individuals in cities, particularly,

00:01:45.520 --> 00:01:50.520
might be electing one representative,

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:54.388
when a rural county, or a
rural geographical area,

00:01:54.388 --> 00:01:57.760
much smaller in terms of population,

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.460
would also have the same amount of votes.

00:02:00.460 --> 00:02:03.600
So there was a disproportionate weight

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.977
to smaller communities
based upon population,

00:02:06.977 --> 00:02:11.720
which had very significant
political consequences

00:02:11.720 --> 00:02:13.910
with respect to allocation of money

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:15.840
and resources and the like.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:20.840
- [Charles] So in the US,
we draw lines as a way of

00:02:20.967 --> 00:02:23.440
aggregating voters together,

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:26.130
so that way we can elect people

00:02:26.130 --> 00:02:29.180
to various representative bodies.

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:32.230
So the process of doing
so, drawing the lines,

00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:34.020
is called, depending on when it's done,

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:37.170
reapportionment or
redistricting or districting.

00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:40.250
So we draw lines through districts

00:02:40.250 --> 00:02:42.950
for the purposes of representation.

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:45.599
And that has been the practice in the US

00:02:45.599 --> 00:02:50.464
for basically time immemorial,
the way of districting

00:02:50.464 --> 00:02:53.692
for the purposes of representation.

00:02:53.692 --> 00:02:57.794
Many states, especially
in the late 19th century

00:02:57.794 --> 00:03:02.480
to early 20th century,
refuse, they drew the lines,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:04.810
in the case of Tennessee,
they drew the line

00:03:04.810 --> 00:03:07.870
in 1901, that was the last
time they reapportioned,

00:03:07.870 --> 00:03:10.960
where they counted the number
of voters in the state,

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:14.850
and then they decided how
many voters were going to be

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.780
in each particular district.

00:03:16.780 --> 00:03:18.678
So a lot of time has passed from 1901,

00:03:18.678 --> 00:03:22.150
when the case was decided in 1961.

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.240
And many people moved from the rural areas

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:29.303
to the urban areas, or
from the rural areas

00:03:29.303 --> 00:03:34.303
to the suburbs, and the
districts were just not equal.

00:03:34.427 --> 00:03:36.380
There weren't the same numbers of voters

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:38.290
in each particular district.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:41.810
- [Kim] So this case took
place originally in Tennessee

00:03:41.810 --> 00:03:44.970
and was brought by a
man named Charles Baker.

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:48.510
So who was Charles Baker
and why did he object to

00:03:48.510 --> 00:03:51.210
how districting was done in Tennessee?

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.940
- [Olson] Well, he was
among a group of citizens.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:57.769
In a way, like many other
Supreme Court cases,

00:03:57.769 --> 00:04:01.047
it's an accident that his name is

00:04:01.047 --> 00:04:03.730
the one by which we remember the case.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:06.410
So he came together
along with some lawyers

00:04:06.410 --> 00:04:09.369
and other citizens in this Tennessee area,

00:04:09.369 --> 00:04:12.551
and brought the case, because they listed

00:04:12.551 --> 00:04:17.551
the participants as
plaintiffs alphabetically,

00:04:17.620 --> 00:04:20.530
Mr. Baker was the first one on the list

00:04:20.530 --> 00:04:22.060
and he became the plaintiff.

00:04:22.060 --> 00:04:24.530
There were a group of
citizens in Tennessee,

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:28.760
he was on the board of a
judicial body in Tennessee,

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:31.914
but he was among several
citizens who objected

00:04:31.914 --> 00:04:36.130
to the way the state
legislature was apportioned.

00:04:36.130 --> 00:04:38.610
- [Charles] Suppose that
we have a classroom,

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:40.400
let's say we have an AP History class.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:43.230
And we're deciding I wanna
take the class at the dinner.

00:04:43.230 --> 00:04:44.570
We're making two decisions,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:46.710
a seafood place or a steak place.

00:04:46.710 --> 00:04:48.820
Let's suppose that we have 10 people

00:04:48.820 --> 00:04:52.410
in the class, and we have three districts.

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:54.740
So we're gonna decide on the basis

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:57.080
of the decisions of those three districts.

00:04:57.080 --> 00:05:00.060
Okay, so we've got one
district of two people

00:05:00.060 --> 00:05:03.210
who like seafood, another
district of two others

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.259
who like seafood, and a
district of six people

00:05:05.259 --> 00:05:07.580
who like steak.

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:08.620
But we're gonna decide

00:05:08.620 --> 00:05:11.050
on the basis of the votes
of each of the districts,

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:13.760
and then the outcome, the majority

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:16.290
of the votes of the district
will decide the outcome.

00:05:16.290 --> 00:05:18.960
So remember the first
district had two people

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:21.180
and both of those people like seafood.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:23.550
Our second district also had two people

00:05:23.550 --> 00:05:24.960
and both of those like seafood.

00:05:24.960 --> 00:05:27.130
And our third district had six people

00:05:27.130 --> 00:05:30.600
and those six like steak.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:33.560
Now, on the basis of the
decision of the district,

00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.050
right, so each district
counts for one vote,

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.320
seafood wins two to one.

00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:41.787
But, if you look at the
total number of people

00:05:41.787 --> 00:05:44.500
and the total preferences,
there are actually

00:05:44.500 --> 00:05:48.890
six people who like steak and
four people who like seafood.

00:05:48.890 --> 00:05:52.640
So really, the decision
should be in favor of steak

00:05:52.640 --> 00:05:55.650
as the majority of the
people, of the voters

00:05:55.650 --> 00:05:58.710
in that classroom preferred
steak over seafood.

00:05:58.710 --> 00:06:01.150
But we can rejigger that outcome by

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:04.310
just dividing the people into districts

00:06:04.310 --> 00:06:07.560
and dividing them into unequal districts

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.420
and then saying each
district gets one vote.

00:06:10.420 --> 00:06:12.100
Well for all intents and purposes,

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:14.140
this is exactly what Tennessee had done.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.610
They basically divided
the state into districts,

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:18.350
so that way you can elect representatives

00:06:18.350 --> 00:06:21.480
from that district, but
some districts only had,

00:06:21.480 --> 00:06:25.330
let's say, 100 people, and
others had 1000 people,

00:06:25.330 --> 00:06:29.630
and some had 400, some
had, you know, 10,000.

00:06:29.630 --> 00:06:31.540
I'm making up the numbers
here, but just to give you

00:06:31.540 --> 00:06:35.140
an example of what the
disparities were like.

00:06:35.140 --> 00:06:37.870
But each counted essentially as one,

00:06:37.870 --> 00:06:41.730
each district had one vote, even though

00:06:41.730 --> 00:06:44.210
they weren't equal in terms
of the number of people

00:06:44.210 --> 00:06:45.530
that were in the districts.

00:06:45.530 --> 00:06:47.020
When a student comes to me

00:06:47.020 --> 00:06:49.310
and says, well wait a minute,
there are many more people

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:52.210
here who prefer steak
since it's the majority.

00:06:52.210 --> 00:06:53.850
I'm not going to change the system

00:06:53.850 --> 00:06:56.560
because the system
reflects my preferences.

00:06:56.560 --> 00:06:58.080
And it was the same thing in Tennessee,

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.100
the state legislature did
not have the incentive

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:03.550
to change the system, because if they did,

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:04.970
they would loose their seats.

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:07.030
- [Kim] So in Supreme
Court cases, of course,

00:07:07.030 --> 00:07:09.390
the Supreme Court is
turning to the Constitution

00:07:09.390 --> 00:07:11.140
for its interpretation.

00:07:11.140 --> 00:07:12.470
So what does the Constitution

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.720
say about apportioning representatives?

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:16.451
Does it make any mention of

00:07:16.451 --> 00:07:18.160
how districts are supposed to be drawn?

00:07:18.160 --> 00:07:20.350
- [Olson] It does not,
it's very interesting

00:07:20.350 --> 00:07:22.983
that our Constitution, as everyone knows,

00:07:22.983 --> 00:07:26.808
gives two senators for each state,

00:07:26.808 --> 00:07:30.355
and then apportions the
House of Representatives

00:07:30.355 --> 00:07:34.621
by district, which is required to be

00:07:34.621 --> 00:07:39.621
redetermined every 10
years, after the Census.

00:07:40.065 --> 00:07:44.690
But prior to Baker versus Carr,
there was very little regard

00:07:44.690 --> 00:07:49.050
to the fact that some
districts were more populous

00:07:49.050 --> 00:07:52.580
than other districts, which
meant that an individual voter

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:56.693
in a populous district
who had the same amount,

00:07:57.827 --> 00:08:00.750
who had a vote that was diluted,

00:08:00.750 --> 00:08:02.990
in a sense, that he shared that vote,

00:08:02.990 --> 00:08:06.720
or that weight of his vote
with many more persons.

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:09.960
And this was rampant all
over the United States,

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:13.023
in California, Los Angeles County,

00:08:14.490 --> 00:08:18.850
which was 40% of the population
of the state of California,

00:08:18.850 --> 00:08:22.640
had one representative
because it was a county

00:08:22.640 --> 00:08:24.520
and it had lots of people in it.

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:26.580
And then there were three smaller counties

00:08:26.580 --> 00:08:31.251
who had very very few people,
maybe had 10 or 15,000 people

00:08:31.251 --> 00:08:35.210
in the county, and they
each had one vote as well.

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.040
- [Kim] Okay so Charles Baker

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:40.003
takes this case to the
courts, and then what happens?

00:08:40.003 --> 00:08:42.760
How did this case make its
way through the courts?

00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.230
- [Charles] Well the one
thing to think about,

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:48.830
the reason why Baker versus
Carr is such an important case,

00:08:48.830 --> 00:08:51.920
we have to remember
that the federal courts,

00:08:51.920 --> 00:08:55.170
in particular the United
States Supreme Court,

00:08:55.170 --> 00:08:57.760
had said in a case called
Colegrove versus Green,

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.160
look we the federal
courts are not going to

00:09:01.160 --> 00:09:03.658
involve ourselves in any of these

00:09:03.658 --> 00:09:05.910
redistricting type cases.

00:09:05.910 --> 00:09:07.890
We don't want to have
anything to do with them,

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:10.340
we don't believe we have
the power to hear them.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:12.630
So in 46, the court said,

00:09:12.630 --> 00:09:13.740
we don't think we have the power to

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.300
hear these cases, and
no matter what happens,

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:21.720
those are cases that belong
to the political process.

00:09:21.720 --> 00:09:25.360
Those are not cases that
belong in the federal court,

00:09:25.360 --> 00:09:27.570
in large part, in important part,

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:29.320
because we don't think the Constitution

00:09:29.320 --> 00:09:32.635
has anything to say about those cases.

00:09:32.635 --> 00:09:34.190
- [Olson] So when it got
to the Supreme Court,

00:09:34.190 --> 00:09:37.390
again in Baker versus
Carr, the question was,

00:09:37.390 --> 00:09:40.980
whether the court would
reverse that earlier decision

00:09:40.980 --> 00:09:43.678
and decide, this is something that we,

00:09:43.678 --> 00:09:46.650
as a court, can deal with as opposed to

00:09:46.650 --> 00:09:50.210
something that must remain
in the political realm.

00:09:50.210 --> 00:09:55.210
And the case was argued
twice, in April of 1961

00:09:58.070 --> 00:10:02.130
and again in October of 1961,

00:10:02.130 --> 00:10:03.980
the court set it for reargument

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.270
because the first time it was argued,

00:10:06.270 --> 00:10:08.740
one of the Justices was undecided,

00:10:08.740 --> 00:10:11.850
and they were divided four to four,

00:10:11.850 --> 00:10:14.830
and they decided to set
it for reargument again.

00:10:14.830 --> 00:10:17.250
And when the case was then decided

00:10:17.250 --> 00:10:19.600
the second after the second argument,

00:10:19.600 --> 00:10:21.450
the court ruled six to two,

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:23.980
one of the Justices became disabled

00:10:23.980 --> 00:10:26.398
at the end of the deliberation,

00:10:26.398 --> 00:10:28.010
so it was only eight rather than nine.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:30.280
But they decided six to two.

00:10:30.280 --> 00:10:32.298
The relatively narrow question,

00:10:32.298 --> 00:10:36.708
not one person, one vote,
but the narrow question,

00:10:36.708 --> 00:10:39.730
is this something that the Constitution

00:10:39.730 --> 00:10:42.600
gives us permission to decide.

00:10:42.600 --> 00:10:46.566
And reciting the 14th
Amendment to the Constitution,

00:10:46.566 --> 00:10:50.170
which contains the well known due process

00:10:50.170 --> 00:10:52.130
and equal protection clause,

00:10:52.130 --> 00:10:54.890
the court in Baker versus Carr decided

00:10:54.890 --> 00:10:57.680
we can look at the Constitution,

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:01.310
discern from the equal protection clause,

00:11:01.310 --> 00:11:03.820
that there is a due process clause,

00:11:03.820 --> 00:11:05.900
but mostly the equal protection clause,

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:09.890
there is a basis for us
to decide that it might

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:13.870
violate an individual's
Constitutional right

00:11:13.870 --> 00:11:17.160
to equal protection of the
laws, if they're placed

00:11:17.160 --> 00:11:20.170
in a political situation where their vote

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:22.700
counts less than someone else's vote.

00:11:22.700 --> 00:11:24.700
- [Charles] And so that's the way

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:26.780
that the court got around that question.

00:11:26.780 --> 00:11:28.850
And the technical legal term

00:11:28.850 --> 00:11:31.600
is to say that these
cases are justiciable,

00:11:31.600 --> 00:11:33.720
which means that courts can hear them,

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.440
that the federal courts have the power

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.240
to decide these types of cases.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.820
Now the court did not
decide the second question

00:11:41.820 --> 00:11:44.760
in Baker versus Carr,
which is okay, suppose that

00:11:44.760 --> 00:11:46.560
that is true, right, if the court says it,

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.404
so it is now true, that federal courts

00:11:49.404 --> 00:11:52.176
do have the power to make those decisions,

00:11:52.176 --> 00:11:54.830
how do we determine what is the standard

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:56.690
that we're going to use to determine

00:11:56.690 --> 00:12:00.070
whether the Constitution is violated.

00:12:00.070 --> 00:12:02.490
And that question, the
court could also decide,

00:12:02.490 --> 00:12:04.210
the court said something
to the affect that,

00:12:04.210 --> 00:12:06.527
look, we know that there are standards,

00:12:06.527 --> 00:12:09.370
they are available, what
we're deciding now is that

00:12:09.370 --> 00:12:12.990
we have the power because
the equal protection clause

00:12:12.990 --> 00:12:15.994
in fact means that the
state can't draw lines

00:12:15.994 --> 00:12:18.550
that are so malapportioned

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:21.620
that they undermine
the equality of voters.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.680
- [Kim] Baker versus Carr
says that the courts then

00:12:24.680 --> 00:12:27.200
can rule on redistricting.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:32.200
Did this bring in a flood of
cases about redistricting?

00:12:33.970 --> 00:12:34.803
- [Charles] It did.

00:12:34.803 --> 00:12:38.560
Once the court decided
that this was fair game,

00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:43.340
that this was appropriate for
courts to consider whether

00:12:43.340 --> 00:12:48.070
or not disparities in
voting power as a result

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.508
of packing people into different
districts in different ways

00:12:52.508 --> 00:12:56.090
constituted a violation
of the Constitution.

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:59.430
Then, in a relatively short
term by Supreme Court standards

00:12:59.430 --> 00:13:04.430
in 1963 and 1964, and other
decisions that all came about

00:13:05.140 --> 00:13:09.510
before the end of the
60s, the court pronounced

00:13:09.510 --> 00:13:12.660
in case after case, that
one person, one vote

00:13:12.660 --> 00:13:17.660
did indeed was required
by the Constitution.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:21.490
So Baker versus Carr opened the door for

00:13:21.490 --> 00:13:24.830
a stream of decisions
from the Supreme Court

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:28.900
that put into our Constitutional fabric,

00:13:28.900 --> 00:13:31.260
the concept of one person, one vote,

00:13:31.260 --> 00:13:35.780
which changed, probably more
than anything, the structure of

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:39.722
our political government
in the United States.

00:13:39.722 --> 00:13:41.060
- [Kim] Do you think that
our election system today

00:13:41.060 --> 00:13:43.830
conforms to this standard
of one person, one vote?

00:13:43.830 --> 00:13:44.910
- [Olson] Well we probably haven't

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:47.780
solved all of the problems,
but compared to where we were

00:13:47.780 --> 00:13:50.750
in 1961, before Baker versus Carr,

00:13:50.750 --> 00:13:52.939
we've really crossed that rubicon.

00:13:52.939 --> 00:13:57.020
Most districts are relatively equal.

00:13:57.020 --> 00:14:00.210
There is a Census every 10 years

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:03.913
so that we can count the
number of persons in districts.

00:14:04.812 --> 00:14:07.540
There are still cases that come along,

00:14:07.540 --> 00:14:10.656
there are cases involving
the allocation of persons

00:14:10.656 --> 00:14:15.656
by race, and whether
there are systems in place

00:14:16.510 --> 00:14:19.380
that discriminate on the basis of race.

00:14:19.380 --> 00:14:20.730
- [Charles] Some states have passed laws

00:14:20.730 --> 00:14:24.370
requiring voters to present
photo identification

00:14:24.370 --> 00:14:27.410
when the voter goes to
vote at the polling place.

00:14:27.410 --> 00:14:31.840
And one objection to the voter photo ID,

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:35.221
is that it's a violation of
the equal protection clause.

00:14:35.221 --> 00:14:38.380
It's a violation of the idea of equality,

00:14:38.380 --> 00:14:41.270
that voters are not being treated equally

00:14:41.270 --> 00:14:44.270
vis-a-vis one another, because
some voters have voter IDs

00:14:44.270 --> 00:14:46.198
and others have a hard time getting them

00:14:46.198 --> 00:14:48.210
for whatever reasons.

00:14:48.210 --> 00:14:50.790
And there might be racial
disproportional effects,

00:14:50.790 --> 00:14:52.710
there might be class effects,

00:14:52.710 --> 00:14:54.080
there might be gender effects.

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.560
And so this raises a violation of

00:14:56.560 --> 00:14:59.830
the equal protection clause,
and these are the types of

00:14:59.830 --> 00:15:03.430
arguments that are now
available to someone

00:15:03.430 --> 00:15:07.310
challenging the problem
of inequality in voting

00:15:07.310 --> 00:15:09.900
after Baker versus Carr that
were not available before

00:15:09.900 --> 00:15:11.140
Baker versus Carr.

00:15:11.140 --> 00:15:12.880
And to fully answer your question,

00:15:12.880 --> 00:15:14.753
some people might say,
look we don't fully have

00:15:14.753 --> 00:15:17.535
one person, one vote
because we have a lot of

00:15:17.535 --> 00:15:21.103
laws that treat voters unequally.

00:15:21.945 --> 00:15:25.290
- [Olson] If the
political party A controls

00:15:25.290 --> 00:15:29.996
both houses of a legislator
in a particular state

00:15:29.996 --> 00:15:34.800
and the person who's in
the Governor's Mansion,

00:15:34.800 --> 00:15:38.046
then those lines can be drawn to favor

00:15:38.046 --> 00:15:42.910
that political party so that
you might have situations

00:15:42.910 --> 00:15:47.179
where a political party
maybe only has 40% of the

00:15:47.179 --> 00:15:50.020
registered voters in a particular state,

00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:54.060
but maybe 55% of the
elected representatives.

00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.440
That's called political gerrymandering,

00:15:56.440 --> 00:16:00.400
and those issues have
come before the court.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:01.790
- [Charles] We still haven't achieved

00:16:01.790 --> 00:16:04.770
the type of equality that was promised

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:07.780
by the Baker line of cases.

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:10.410
- [Kim] So we've learned
that in Baker versus Carr,

00:16:10.410 --> 00:16:12.730
the Supreme Court ruled that redistricting

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:16.300
was a justiciable issue,
or something that the

00:16:16.300 --> 00:16:18.010
federal courts could decide.

00:16:18.010 --> 00:16:20.890
Because ensuring that
votes have equal weight

00:16:20.890 --> 00:16:24.271
is important to ensuring equal
protection under the law.

00:16:24.271 --> 00:16:27.197
The standard of one person, one vote

00:16:27.197 --> 00:16:29.780
opened the flood gate for cases about

00:16:29.780 --> 00:16:32.220
equality of representation
and districting,

00:16:32.220 --> 00:16:35.610
which are still being litigated
at the Supreme Court today.

00:16:35.610 --> 00:16:37.700
To learn more about Baker versus Carr,

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.780
visit the National Constitution Center's

00:16:39.780 --> 00:16:42.940
Interactive Constitution,
and Khan Academy's resources

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:44.753
on US government and politics.

